Calls for a new stadium grow as Cheongju Stadium reaches its limit

Kim Young-hwan &ldquo;Considering multipurpose dome stadium project&rdquo;

Lee Beom-seok &ldquo;Fulfill baseball stadium pledge&rdquo;

&nbsp;

Local politicians &ldquo;Osong is the optimal transportation hub&rdquo;

National politicians &ldquo;Challenging, but open to cooperation&rdquo;



<img alt="As the aging Cheongju Baseball Stadium in Chungbuk’s Cheongju can no longer host professional baseball games, discussions are underway to replace it with a multipurpose dome stadium and to expand the region’s sports and cultural infrastructure. The photo shows a Hanwha Eagles fan cheering passionately during a 2024 KBO League game between the Hanwha Eagles and the Kiwoom Heroes at Cheongju Baseball Stadium. (Source: Newsis)" src="https://www.newscj.com/news/photo/202506/3284309_3352116_5436.png" />
As the aging Cheongju Baseball Stadium in Chungbuk&rsquo;s Cheongju can no longer host professional baseball games, discussions are underway to replace it with a multipurpose dome stadium and to expand the region&rsquo;s sports and cultural infrastructure. The photo shows a Hanwha Eagles fan cheering passionately during a 2024 KBO League game between the Hanwha Eagles and the Kiwoom Heroes at Cheongju Baseball Stadium. (Source: Newsis)



[Cheonji Ilbo Chungbuk=Reporter Kim Heung-soon] Could a multipurpose dome stadium be built in Cheongju, Chungbuk? As Cheongju Baseball Stadium no longer meets the criteria to host professional games, discussions around upgrading the region&rsquo;s sports infrastructure have rapidly gained momentum. Completed in 1979, the stadium has undergone several renovations, but due to aging facilities and limited capacity, it has repeatedly failed to meet the standards set by the Korea Baseball Organization (KBO).

The breaking point came when the Hanwha Eagles excluded all home games in Cheongju for the 2024 season. Citing concerns about player safety, reduced performance, and poor fan accessibility, the team&rsquo;s decision sparked a public outcry. Residents began to agree: &ldquo;Whether through renovations or a new stadium, something must be done.&rdquo;

In response, on June 5, Governor Kim Young-hwan met with Park No-jun, president of Woosuk University, at the provincial government&rsquo;s open forum and expressed his will to build &ldquo;a multipurpose dome stadium that can host not only baseball, but also concerts, exhibitions, soccer games, and other cultural and sporting events.&rdquo;

The idea has since evolved from a simple facility expansion to a massive urban development project. It also aligns with President Lee Jae-myung&rsquo;s campaign pledge to build the &lsquo;Cheongju Sports Complex,&rsquo; further adding political momentum. That proposal includes constructing a large baseball stadium, soccer field, exhibition hall, and concert venue on an 80,000-square-meter site.

&lsquo;Osong&rsquo; has emerged as the most promising candidate for the stadium&rsquo;s location, with its unmatched transportation infrastructure including a KTX hub and three major expressways. Its growing bio and medical sectors also support its potential as a multi-industry cluster combining sports, industry, and tourism.

◆ Existing Stadium Can&rsquo;t Keep Up With the Times


<img alt="Lim Byung-woon (People Power Party, Cheongju-7), a member of the Chungbuk Provincial Council, proposed during a five-minute speech at the second plenary session of the 425th regular meeting on April 30 that Osong should be developed into a sports cluster to ensure the future of Chungbuk’s sports industry. (Provided by: Chungbuk Provincial Council) ©Cheonji Ilbo, June 23, 2025." src="https://www.newscj.com/news/photo/202506/3284309_3352117_5556.png" />
Lim Byung-woon (People Power Party, Cheongju-7), a member of the Chungbuk Provincial Council, proposed during a five-minute speech at the second plenary session of the 425th regular meeting on April 30 that Osong should be developed into a sports cluster to ensure the future of Chungbuk&rsquo;s sports industry. (Provided by: Chungbuk Provincial Council) &copy;Cheonji Ilbo, June 23, 2025.



At the 425th Chungbuk Provincial Assembly session on April 30, provincial lawmaker Lim Byung-woon (People Power Party, Cheongju-7) proposed developing a sports cluster centered on Osong. &ldquo;The sports industry is now a complex sector involving the economy, tourism, and job creation,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;Chungbuk should foster it as a strategic industry.&rdquo;

Amid these developments, there are reports of efforts to attract an existing pro baseball team rather than creating a new one. One insider said the province is working on relocating a franchise from another region, while another mentioned plans to visit baseball-advanced areas abroad for benchmarking.

Earlier in March, Cheongju and Chungbuk officials met with veteran baseball figures including former Hanwha Eagles manager Kim Eung-ryong to discuss local baseball development. However, key sources say no concrete plans have yet been drafted by either the city or province.

A local politician even suggested financing the stadium by linking it to a private apartment development project. Some have proposed using land near the current Sajik-dong stadium or developing new zones in Osong to spread the financial burden. Yet there&rsquo;s tension between those urging caution&mdash;&ldquo;Don&rsquo;t rely solely on tax money&rdquo;&mdash;and critics who warn that &ldquo;public facilities should not become tools for real estate gains.&rdquo;

◆ &ldquo;The Need for a New Stadium Is Now Undeniable&rdquo;


<img alt="Governor Kim Young-hwan of Chungbuk (far right) and Cheongju Mayor Lee Beom-seok (center) pose for a commemorative photo following a meeting on March 28 with baseball veterans, including former Hanwha Eagles manager Kim Eung-ryong (second from right), to discuss the allocation of Hanwha’s home games in Cheongju. (Provided by: Cheongju City Hall)" src="https://www.newscj.com/news/photo/202506/3284309_3352118_5635.jpg" />
Governor Kim Young-hwan of Chungbuk (far right) and Cheongju Mayor Lee Beom-seok (center) pose for a commemorative photo following a meeting on March 28 with baseball veterans, including former Hanwha Eagles manager Kim Eung-ryong (second from right), to discuss the allocation of Hanwha&rsquo;s home games in Cheongju. (Provided by: Cheongju City Hall)



Cheongju Baseball Stadium has been a regional hub for over 45 years, but time has caught up with it. With just 9,586 seats and outdated dimensions (100m left/right field, 115m center), the stadium is no longer fit for professional games. Over 17 billion KRW (about $12 million USD) has been invested in renovations since 2007, yet on-site concerns remain: &ldquo;It&rsquo;s still a place where injuries are common.&rdquo;

The Hanwha Eagles have already moved into a new stadium in Daejeon and maintain a second team facility in Seosan, reducing their need for Cheongju games. The two pre-season games held in Cheongju this year were widely seen as a farewell.

Long-time resident Kim Sang-tae (75, male) who has lived in Sajik-dong for over 40 years said, &ldquo;A dome stadium built here would boost local business and overall development&mdash;it would help more than hurt.&rdquo; But an opposing view came from a man in his 80s who argued, &ldquo;The stadium is empty most of the time, even for youth games. It&rsquo;s a waste of money.&rdquo;

The local baseball community sees the issue as part of a broader shift from &lsquo;watching baseball&rsquo; to &lsquo;experiencing baseball.&rsquo; Oh Jong-hyuk, president of the Chungbuk Baseball Softball Association, explained, &ldquo;It&rsquo;s not just about stadium size anymore. Fans now want comfort zones, interactive zones, and places to enjoy alongside watching.&rdquo; He added, &ldquo;If we adapt to this trend, Cheongju&rsquo;s stadium won&rsquo;t be sidelined.&rdquo; He also proposed: &ldquo;Let&rsquo;s use the current Cheongju stadium for youth and high school training, and build a new dome for professional games and culture.&rdquo;

◆ Cooperation Between Governments and Realistic Roadmap Needed


<img alt="On the 16th, Kim Byung-ki, acting party leader and floor leader of the Democratic Party of Korea, speaks during a Supreme Council meeting held at the National Assembly. (Source: Yonhap News)" src="https://www.newscj.com/news/photo/202506/3284309_3352119_5823.jpg" />
On the 16th, Kim Byung-ki, acting party leader and floor leader of the Democratic Party of Korea, speaks during a Supreme Council meeting held at the National Assembly. (Source: Yonhap News)



To make these visions reality, cooperation between local and central governments is essential. Lawmaker Song Jae-bong (Cheongju, Cheongwon) said, &ldquo;This is not a project easily funded by the central government,&rdquo; but added, &ldquo;If the local government presents a detailed plan, we&rsquo;ll work together to make it happen.&rdquo;

President Lee Jae-myung&rsquo;s campaign promise of the Cheongju Sports Complex could provide the breakthrough Chungbuk needs. But for the plan to move into the execution phase, detailed site selection, annual usage planning, and long-term funding strategies must be established. Most importantly, any plan must account for public demand and long-term sustainability.

The &lsquo;dome stadium&rsquo; isn&rsquo;t just a sports facility&mdash;it could become a symbol of Chungbuk&rsquo;s cultural identity and urban brand. Even without a dome, any upgraded facility would go a long way in addressing the sports infrastructure gap that residents currently feel.

Just as Daegu and Gwangju leveraged stadiums to spur local economies, tourism, and youth employment, now is the time for Cheongju and Chungbuk to build a realistic, cooperative plan and seek active support from the Lee Jae-myung administration.
